1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,320
The reason they're tricky is because there is an assumption, like you regret doing A,

2
00:00:06,320 --> 00:00:10,000
and you think if only I'd done B, things would be better.

3
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:11,360
But you don't know that that's true.

4
00:00:11,360 --> 00:00:15,920
You might say, boy, I regret starting that company that failed because it was a failure.

5
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:18,720
Well, yeah, but it led to something much bigger.

6
00:00:18,720 --> 00:00:20,560
You learned so much.

7
00:00:20,560 --> 00:00:27,120
Now, maybe if you'd said instead of starting that company that failed, maybe I took this

8
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:31,520
high-paying job and I worked my way up through the corporate organization,

9
00:00:32,160 --> 00:00:39,040
and you'd be sitting at some high executive level in this corporation and looking back and saying,

10
00:00:39,040 --> 00:00:42,400
well, am I glad I didn't do that startup that failed?

11
00:00:43,200 --> 00:00:45,360
And yet you're so much further ahead now.

12
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:47,600
So who knows?

13
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:51,840
Who knows what choice you made that appears to be the wrong choice

14
00:00:52,480 --> 00:00:54,080
as to whether it really was?

15
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:55,360
Maybe it was exactly right.

16
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:56,640
Maybe things would have turned out better.

17
00:00:56,640 --> 00:00:57,440
Maybe they wouldn't.

18
00:00:57,440 --> 00:01:00,400
So I'm very hesitant to look back on.

19
00:01:00,400 --> 00:01:05,760
There are many things I can look back on and say, gee, I do wonder what if I'd gone

20
00:01:05,760 --> 00:01:07,920
down the right path instead of the left path?

21
00:01:07,920 --> 00:01:09,280
What would that have looked like?

22
00:01:10,240 --> 00:01:13,920
But there's no guarantees it would look better, and my life has been pretty damn good.

23
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,480
So in that sense, I have no regrets.

24
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:20,480
Two regrets I do have, very personal regrets.

25
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:22,560
I've never shared these publicly.

26
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:30,000
When I was a kid, my father was a very handy guy.

27
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:33,440
He loved building things, working on the house, that kind of stuff.

28
00:01:34,800 --> 00:01:40,080
I was not that kind of kid, and I don't know, I was 8 or 10 years old at one point.

29
00:01:40,080 --> 00:01:46,560
And for my birthday or Christmas, I don't remember, he bought me a jigsaw,

30
00:01:47,440 --> 00:01:51,280
which is, for people who don't know, it's an electric saw.

31
00:01:51,280 --> 00:01:55,840
It's got a little blade that goes up and allows you to cut wood in very fine kinds of patterns.

32
00:01:57,440 --> 00:02:00,080
Last thing in the world this kid wanted.

33
00:02:02,480 --> 00:02:05,600
And I let my dad know, and he was crushed.

34
00:02:06,480 --> 00:02:14,640
Because for him, it was the best gift he could possibly think of to give to an 8 or 10 year old or whatever it was.

35
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:19,440
And so one of the regrets, and I give myself some grace because I was very young, and

36
00:02:20,400 --> 00:02:26,000
reasonably you could expect that I didn't have the maturity to deal with it the way I would have.

37
00:02:26,000 --> 00:02:34,320
But I do regret, because I could see the pain in his face when I kind of rejected that gift.

38
00:02:36,400 --> 00:02:41,120
And maybe that taught me a good lesson in being more empathetic going forward.

39
00:02:41,120 --> 00:02:42,880
So again, do I really regret it?

40
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:44,880
Well, I regret that I hurt my father.

41
00:02:45,120 --> 00:02:49,760
But I learned something pretty valuable.

42
00:02:49,760 --> 00:02:52,080
And you've remembered that for 70 years?

43
00:02:52,080 --> 00:02:54,080
I've remembered that for 70 years, yeah.

44
00:02:56,000 --> 00:02:59,120
I've got similar stories of ways I reacted as a kid.

45
00:02:59,120 --> 00:03:00,240
I think most people do.

46
00:03:02,960 --> 00:03:07,680
And then my second one, and this is even bigger, I was 24 when my dad died.

47
00:03:08,960 --> 00:03:13,920
And he died of emphysema and slow lingering death.

48
00:03:13,920 --> 00:03:15,040
He died in the hospital.

49
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:22,160
And the night before he died, the day before he died, I was visiting him.

50
00:03:23,040 --> 00:03:26,160
And he was sitting on the edge of the bed.

51
00:03:28,800 --> 00:03:34,160
And he said to me, I'm going to die now.

52
00:03:36,080 --> 00:03:38,880
You know, I'm going to die tonight.

53
00:03:39,680 --> 00:03:41,280
Turns out, of course, he was right.

54
00:03:41,280 --> 00:03:41,840
He did.

55
00:03:41,840 --> 00:03:43,040
That was the night he died.

56
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:54,560
And instead of recognizing that this was a moment where he wanted to talk to his son about this,

57
00:03:56,400 --> 00:04:00,880
this probably the most momentous event that any of us will ever face,

58
00:04:00,880 --> 00:04:03,680
momentous event that any of us will ever face,

59
00:04:06,880 --> 00:04:11,760
instead of recognizing that, I went to the typical trope of,

60
00:04:11,760 --> 00:04:13,680
Dad, don't talk like that.

61
00:04:13,680 --> 00:04:14,480
You're not going to die.

62
00:04:14,480 --> 00:04:15,840
You've got a long way to go.

63
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:17,040
You're going to be fine.

64
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:18,560
I went to all that bullshit.

65
00:04:19,840 --> 00:04:24,720
Instead of just recognizing, whether he was right or wrong,

66
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:32,800
that he was facing a momentous thing and he didn't want to hear,

67
00:04:33,840 --> 00:04:36,560
don't think about that, think more positively.

68
00:04:36,560 --> 00:04:43,920
He wanted to share with his son what he was facing.

69
00:04:45,440 --> 00:04:50,960
And I regret that I wasn't there for him in that moment,

70
00:04:51,520 --> 00:04:56,080
but I regret that I didn't get to experience that with him in that moment.

71
00:04:57,040 --> 00:04:58,560
So that's my biggest.

72
00:05:02,240 --> 00:05:04,320
I can still see it still in your face.

73
00:05:07,920 --> 00:05:09,600
That was 50 years ago.

74
00:05:13,440 --> 00:05:17,360
Is there a reason why you think in that moment you didn't

75
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:20,080
want to go in that direction?

76
00:05:20,080 --> 00:05:21,360
To go in that direction with him?

77
00:05:22,000 --> 00:05:26,080
Wasn't a matter of what I wanted because it's not like I considered

78
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:29,680
I can either blow it off, which is what I did,

79
00:05:30,320 --> 00:05:32,080
or embrace it and go there with him.

80
00:05:32,080 --> 00:05:33,760
I didn't even think that way.

81
00:05:33,760 --> 00:05:38,160
It's not like I made the wrong choice.

82
00:05:38,880 --> 00:05:42,480
I wasn't mature enough to recognize there was a choice.

83
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:48,960
I wasn't mature enough to recognize the real dynamic of what was happening.

84
00:05:48,960 --> 00:05:50,160
And for that, you deserve grace.

85
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:52,720
Thank you.

86
00:05:52,720 --> 00:05:55,920
And I agree with that, but I still regret it.

87
00:05:55,920 --> 00:06:03,680
Because how much better for both of us would it have been if I had recognized it?

88
00:06:04,480 --> 00:06:05,920
JL, we have a closing tradition on this

89
00:06:05,920 --> 00:06:07,840
podcast where the last guest leaves a question for the next.

90
00:06:09,120 --> 00:06:15,040
What is something that you think is true that you haven't yet been able to validate?

91
00:06:15,840 --> 00:06:24,320
I think at this point in my life, I feel pretty comfortable about what I think is true.

92
00:06:27,360 --> 00:06:33,520
I'm not sure this answers the question, but a good example is

93
00:06:34,560 --> 00:06:37,600
I am pretty sure that there is no afterlife.

94
00:06:40,480 --> 00:06:43,200
I have a high degree of confidence in that.

95
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:46,080
But of course, as the song once said,

96
00:06:46,080 --> 00:06:48,640
we'll never know by living, and only our dying will tell.

97
00:06:49,520 --> 00:06:53,680
And I am very curious about death.

98
00:06:54,640 --> 00:07:00,960
I am very curious as to what is on the other side, if anything.

99
00:07:02,080 --> 00:07:07,440
So in a perverse way, I guess, I am looking forward to my death.

100
00:07:08,800 --> 00:07:10,320
I don't want to get there too soon.

101
00:07:10,960 --> 00:07:13,360
As long as I am mentally and physically capable,

102
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:14,720
I am happy to continue living.

103
00:07:14,720 --> 00:07:15,520
Thank you very much.

104
00:07:16,240 --> 00:07:19,200
But I do have a great curiosity about death.

105
00:07:19,200 --> 00:07:23,520
And I am almost 100% sure that when I am dead, that is just it.

106
00:07:23,520 --> 00:07:24,080
It is over.

107
00:07:25,600 --> 00:07:26,800
But I am curious.

108
00:07:26,800 --> 00:07:30,160
